  "question": {
    "text": "An ideal capacitor of capacitance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.2</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;F</mi></math> is charged to a potential difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The charging battery is then disconnected. The capacitor is then connected to an ideal inductor of self inductance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mH</mi></math>. The current at a time when the potential difference across the capacitor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> is:",
    "image": null
  },
